---
export const prerender = true;

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const SEAT_MAILTO =
  'mailto:library@schooltrusts.net?subject=Board%20or%20Law%20Librarian%20service';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Open seats · America's School Trust Library"
  description="Three seats are open at America's School Trust Library: two Directors and the Law Librarian. What each seat is for, what it asks, what it does not ask, and how to say yes."
  sourceId="participate-open-seats"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    <p class="kicker">Participate</p>
    <h1>Three open seats</h1>

    <p class="doc-meta">
      Two Director seats and the Law Librarian. Postings published September
      10, 2026 under the board's written consent of that date; the Law
      Librarian's role charter travels with its posting as a draft the board
      has not yet adopted.
    </p>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is an Oregon nonprofit public benefit
      corporation, Oregon Registry No. 259005891, formed in July 2026. It
      collects, verifies and publishes the record of the school trust lands the
      United States granted to the states at statehood &mdash; what was
      granted, what was promised, what happened to it, and what the courts have
      said about it. It publishes in the open, corrects in public, and keeps a
      numbered <a href="/about/corrections/log/">corrections ledger</a> that
      anyone can read.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three seats are open: <strong>two Directors</strong> and the
      <strong>Law Librarian</strong>. All three are one-year terms,
      uncompensated and renewable, and all three require a signed annual
      disclosure under the Library's
      <a href="/governance/">Conflict of Interest Policy</a>. The board meets
      at least quarterly and works by written consent between meetings. The
      Library has not
      yet filed its application for federal tax-exempt recognition; it is being
      prepared. No federal tax-exemption determination has been issued, and
      until one is, the Library does not describe contributions as
      tax-deductible. The Library is not asking anyone for money; it asks for
      documents and hands.
    </p>

    <p>
      If one of these describes you, or describes someone you know, write to
      the Secretary, Kenneth Magee, at
      <a href={SEAT_MAILTO}>library@schooltrusts.net</a>. Two paragraphs is
      plenty. There is no form.
    </p>

    <h2>Director &mdash; two seats</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>What the seats are for.</strong> The board is three people: a
      President who also holds the Treasurer's office, a Secretary, and one
      Director. Three is enough to act and too few to disagree usefully. These
      two seats exist so that the Library's decisions are made by people who
      did not build it, and so that the founder can be outvoted.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The discipline the Library most lacks.</strong> One of the two
      seats should go to someone who can take the Treasurer's office &mdash; a
      person at home with accounts, budgets, tax filings and charitable
      reports. The President holds that office today under the board's
      written finding that the arrangement is temporary and necessary; it
      ends when a fourth director is seated or another Treasurer is elected,
      and no later than the election of officers in 2027. The bylaws also
      require, once the board reaches five, at least one educator and at least
      one librarian, archivist, records professional or equivalent collection
      steward; the other seat would ideally bring one of those.
    </p>

    <p><strong>What it asks.</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Read the thing before voting on it. The Library's papers are short by
        policy; nothing put to this board should take more than an hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        Say what you actually think in the room, and let it be recorded when
        you dissent.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sign an annual disclosure of interests, and recuse without being asked
        when one applies.
      </li>
      <li>
        Name two or three real people, once a year, for seats, council, or
        collections. Recruiting is part of the seat, not a favour.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <strong>What it does not ask.</strong> No fundraising quota. No
      litigation. The Library's adopted <a href="/rights/">Constitution</a>
      says it takes no position in any litigation, and directors are not asked
      to take one either. No public advocacy in the Library's name unless you
      choose to.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Who this fits.</strong> Someone who has sat on a board that
      governed rather than applauded &mdash; a trustee, a school board member,
      a librarian or archivist with governance experience, a lawyer who has
      been the one to say &ldquo;not yet.&rdquo; Subject-matter expertise in
      school trust lands is welcome and is not required.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>One thing to know before you say yes.</strong> The Library
      publishes its own mistakes with numbers on them. If a corrections ledger
      that names the institution's errors in public would embarrass you, this
      is the wrong board.
    </p>

    <h2>Law Librarian</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>What the seat is for.</strong> The Library publishes claims about
      what courts have held. The Law Librarian is the person who can say, on
      the record, that a citation has been checked against the reporter
      &mdash; and who is accountable when one has not been. The bylaws make
      the seat a professional role, not automatically an officer or director
      seat.
    </p>

    <p><strong>What it asks.</strong></p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Own the citation standard: every claim tied to a primary source, every
        pin verified against the official reporter, nothing published on a
        secondary source's say-so.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sit as the second signature on the Verified shelf, so that no item
        reaches it on one person's reading.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take the corrections that come in from outside, decide them on the
        merits, and credit the person who sent them.
      </li>
      <li>Sign an annual disclosure.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <strong>What it does not ask.</strong> No litigation support. No
      brief-writing. No opinion on the merits of any pending case. The Library
      provides legal information, never legal advice.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Who this fits.</strong> A law librarian, a reference or
      government-documents librarian, a legal-research instructor, a retired
      appellate clerk, a paralegal with a citation habit. Public-lands or
      trust-law background is a bonus, not a filter. A person who would enjoy
      finding this Library wrong is precisely the person for this seat.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>One thing to know before you say yes.</strong> The Library's
      method uses AI assistance and says so on every page. That method has
      produced real work and it has also produced real errors &mdash;
      quotations that were not in the opinion, a holding published backwards,
      a correction that reached six of the twelve pages citing a case and
      stopped. This
      seat exists because that method needs a human check with a name on it.
      If you think the method is unsound, say so in your two paragraphs; that
      is a useful answer and it will be read as one.
    </p>

    <h2>Role charter &mdash; Law Librarian</h2>

    <p class="doc-meta">
      Draft. The board has not yet adopted this charter; it adopts it, with or
      without change, before or with the appointment. It is published with the
      posting so that a candidate can read what the seat would hold.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Purpose.</strong> The Law Librarian is the Library's standing
        check on the accuracy of its legal citations and the integrity of its
        verified holdings. The office exists so that &ldquo;verified&rdquo;
        means a named person verified it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Appointment and term.</strong> Appointed by the board for a
        one-year renewable term. Uncompensated. Not a director; may attend any
        board meeting and must be given notice of any item touching the
        Verified shelf, the corrections ledger, or the citation standard.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Duties.</strong>
        <ol type="a">
          <li>
            <em>The citation standard.</em> Maintain and publish the standard
            by which a claim is admitted: primary source identified, pin cite
            verified against the official reporter, subsequent history stated,
            and any secondary source labelled as such.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Second signature.</em> No item enters the Verified shelf on
            one reading. The Law Librarian is the second, and may decline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>The corrections ledger.</em> Receive corrections from any
            source, decide them on the merits, publish the disposition with a
            number, and credit the sender by name unless the sender asks
            otherwise.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Propagation.</em> When a correction is made, satisfy
            themselves that it has reached every surface citing the item
            &mdash; not only the page that prompted it. The Library has failed
            this test more than once and the failure is the reason this duty
            is written down.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Annual note.</em> One page a year to the board: what was
            checked, what was found, what remains unverified.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Authority.</strong> The Law Librarian may withhold an item
        from the Verified shelf, may require that a correction be published,
        and may record a dissent in the ledger that the board does not remove.
        The board may overrule a withholding only by recorded vote, with the
        Law Librarian's reasons published alongside.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Limits.</strong> No authority over acquisitions, editorial
        direction, or the Library's public positions. Takes no position on any
        pending litigation, consistent with the Library's Constitution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Disclosure and recusal.</strong> Signs the annual disclosure
        required of directors and officers. Recuses from any item touching a
        source, institution or person in which they have an interest.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Vacancy.</strong> If the seat is vacant, the Verified shelf
        does not grow. That is the point of it.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to say yes</h2>

    <p>
      Write to the Secretary, Kenneth Magee, at
      <a href={SEAT_MAILTO}>library@schooltrusts.net</a>. Say which seat
      interests you and what you would bring to it, and the board will answer.
      The lighter ways in &mdash; a source, a correction, a state to watch
      &mdash; are on the <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a> page; the
      invitation with a card for each kind of reader is
      <a href="/participate/ways-to-help/">Ways to Help</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="colophon-note">
      Postings drafted September 5, 2026 and published September 10, 2026 under
      the board's written consent of September 10, 2026. Drafted with an AI system
      for the Library's board, from the Library's own record, and checked
      against that record by people.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

